## What happened

In **.NET 11**, **DSA was removed from macOS**. Calling `DSA.Create()` (and any DSA sign/verify operation) now **throws at runtime on macOS**. Windows and Linux are unaffected — DSA still works there.

## How to fix

Since you need to **verify legacy signatures**, you have two options:

### Option 1 — Migrate to a modern algorithm (recommended)

Replace DSA with **ECDsa** or **RSA** for new signatures, and re-sign any legacy data:

```csharp
// Before (breaks on macOS)
using var dsa = DSA.Create();

// After
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
var valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### Option 2 — Platform-guard legacy verification

If you **must** verify existing DSA signatures and can't re-sign, gate the DSA path to Windows/Linux only:

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException(
        "DSA signature verification is not supported on macOS with .NET 11+.");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
```

> **Bottom line:** DSA is a legacy algorithm. The long-term fix is to migrate your signing scheme to **ECDsa** or **RSA** and re-sign any data that currently uses DSA signatures.
